<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?><kml xmlns="http://www.opengis.net/kml/2.2"><Document><name>ILLUSTRATIVE starship-flight-15 ascent (3DOF sim)</name><description>ILLUSTRATIVE ASCENT ECEF — Starship Flight 15 / Starlink Group 31-2 (OLP-2, Starbase) — NOT OFFICIAL TELEMETRY
Method: first-principles 3DOF sim (/workspace/trajectory-deck/sim/ascent3dof.py): WGS-84 rotating Earth, J2, US76 drag,
TIMELINE mode: every ignition/cutoff/separation/fairing time is a published hard event; mdot=F_vac/(Isp_vac*g0); thrust SL/vac on ambient pressure.
FITTED (least squares vs published knots): vertical rise 5.81 s, kick 0.417 deg over 10 s, Cd 0.400, payload 51410 kg,
gravity turn, then linear-tangent steering tan(theta)=-0.3208--0.002017*(t-t_ign stage 1).
Vehicle config: SpaceX Starship V3 (Super Heavy 33×Raptor 3 + Ship 3 SL + 3 RVac Raptor 3) — TIMELINE mode (sim/vehicles/starship_v3.json; every number carries a source URL, derivation, or ASSUMPTION).
Starship V3 from SpaceX technical specifications (https://www.spacex.com/vehicles/starship/technical-specifications): Super Heavy 3,650 t propellant, 8,240 tf / 18.1 Mlbf, 9 m; Ship 1,600 t propellant, 1,614 tf, 9 m. Raptor 3 sea-level 250 tf (same page and SpaceX &#x27;Introducing Starship V3&#x27; update, 12 May 2026, https://www.spacex.com/updates/starship-v3). RVac 275 tf (same 12 May 2026 update, up from 258 tf). Engine counts: 33 Raptor 3 on Super Heavy; Ship 3 sea-level + 3 RVac (SpaceX specs page; F14 official narrative: all six on ascent).
Previous file used 1,600 tf (rounded Wikipedia &#x27;initial thrust&#x27;) and a single 350 s Isp for all six ship engines, held constant for 348 s, which left only 9.1 t residual and could not fly the published 20 s insertion. Units were already correct (1,600/1,614 tf is the ship, Super Heavy is 8,240 tf). This revision uses official 1,614 tf and blended 364.72 s Isp; residual after ascent is ~60 t. Insertion is the published 20 s burn with thrust solved to a 275 km perigee (labelled Raptor throttle), not a residual-limited 12.8 s at 250 tf.
TIMELINE mode uses SpaceX&#x27;s published Flight 15 event times (content.spacex.com/api/spacex-website/missions/starship-flight-15): MECO 139 s, hot-stage / ship ignition 141 s, ship ascent cutoff 489 s. Boostback and catch are not modelled; unburned booster propellant stands in for the recovery reserve and is not delivered to the ship.
The 20 s orbital insertion burn (T+26:10–26:30) and payload deploy (start 35:01, complete 1:17:40) are flown in post_cutoff. SpaceX F15: ~275 km, ~14 orbits, &gt;22-hour flight, with a passively safe suborbital opportunity before raising to orbital altitude (same architecture as F14). F14 official: single sea-level Raptor insertion after a passively safe suborbital trajectory.
PRE-FLIGHT ILLUSTRATIVE. Starship Flight 15 / Starlink Group 31-2, 26 V3 sats, OLP-2 Starbase, T0 2026-11-13T22:45:00Z (Fri Nov 13 2:45 p.m. PST), 75-minute window, LL2 TBC pending regulatory approval. First attempt to catch both Super Heavy V3 and the ship at the launch site.
Ascent event times are SpaceX&#x27;s published F15 timeline (MECO 139 s, hot-stage 141 s, ship cutoff 489 s). The 20 s orbital insertion burn (26:10–26:30) is a single sea-level Raptor 3 at 250 tf (F14 official analog: &#x27;reignited a single Raptor sea-level engine&#x27;), flown open-loop for the published 20 s; payload deploy start 35:01, complete 1:17:40. Ship is then scheduled to fly ~14 orbits and attempt RTLS catch (deorbit T+21:35:57) — deorbit/catch are not in this file, which ends at deploy complete.
Ascent target is a passively safe suborbital ellipse (−40 × 275 km stand-in) at T+489 s, 1081 s before apogee, so the published insertion epoch is at first apogee. SpaceX F15 official text uses that architecture. 180 × 275 km (previous file) was the wrong energy class.
Target (see launch config): insertion 163 km, apoapsis 275 km, fpa 0, launch azimuth 107.53 deg; payload 51410 kg.
Pad: 25.996770N -97.157990E. Frame: WGS-84 ECEF, positions km, velocities km/s (Earth-relative).
T0 used: 2026-11-13T22:45:00Z. Re-run with another T0 by shifting utc_iso only; ECEF geometry is Earth-fixed.
POST-CUTOFF: after final cutoff T+489.0 s the stage coasts (same WGS-84/J2/US76-drag model) to PAYLOAD DEPLOY T+4660 s; rows tagged -coast/-burn.
POST-CUTOFF burn &#x27;Orbital insertion burn&#x27;: ign T+1570.0 s, 20.0 s at 1028.65 kN prograde (Isp 350 s); Open-loop: published 20 s insertion on a single sea-level Raptor 3 (F14 official analog), throttled to 1,028,650 N (42% of 250 tf) so the burn supplies the 89.8 m/s needed to circularize the post-coast −31.8 × 269.3 km ellipse. Full 250 tf × 20 s would be ~200 m/s and would loft apogee. The ~6 km shortfall vs SpaceX’s ~275 km is J2/drag on the 18-minute coast from the −40 × 275 km ascent target.
POST-CUTOFF deploy T+4660 s (SpaceX Flight 15 timeline: deploy start T+00:35:01, complete T+01:17:40): alt 261.06 km, osculating 254.8 x 278.4 km, i 31.616 deg.
POST-CUTOFF note: SpaceX F15 official: passively safe suborbital opportunity, then raise to ~275 km. Insertion is the published 20 s single-SL-Raptor burn with thrust solved to a 275 km perigee (labelled throttle). Residuals vs 275 km circular are reported, not hidden.
POST-CUTOFF note: Deorbit burn T+21:35:57 and ship catch T+22:32:34 (SpaceX F15 timeline) are not modelled; file ends at deploy complete T+01:17:40. Leftover ship propellant after insertion stands in for those unpublished later burns.</description>
<Placemark><name>Pad (starship-flight-15)</name><Point><coordinates>-97.1579900,25.9967700,0</coordinates></Point></Placemark>
